# config/Kconfig.security — Secure boot, link encryption, PMP, stack hardening
#
# RFC-0026 Phase C1.  Maps to cargo features `secure-boot-enforced` and
# the RFC-0019 link-encrypt opt-in flag.

menu "Security"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Secure boot
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

config SECURE_BOOT_ENFORCED
    # cargo-feature: secure-boot-enforced
    bool "Enforce Ed25519 signature verification at boot" if !BUILD_TYPE_CERTIFIABLE
    default y if BUILD_TYPE_CERTIFIABLE
    default n
    help
      When enabled, the bootloader verifies the kernel image signature
      using the Ed25519 public key embedded at build time (crates/core/ota, was SECURE_BOOT_KEY_FP -- removed 2026-09-26, V2.6, never consumed)
      before jumping to _start.  If the signature is absent or invalid,
      boot halts unconditionally.
      Disable only for development.  MUST be y for any production
      deployment — enforced by cargo feature `secure-boot-enforced`.
      A BUILD_TYPE_CERTIFIABLE build has it on and cannot turn it off.

config LINK_AUTH_ENFORCED
    # cargo-feature: link-auth-enforced
    bool "Refuse to boot without a valid brain-link key (/fat/LINK.KEY)"
    default n
    depends on USE_ROBOT
    help
      Robot domain only (wave 11): the brain link exists only there.
      The brain<->kernel HMAC envelope keys off a 32-byte pre-shared key
      read from /fat/LINK.KEY at boot.  With this option n, a missing or
      malformed key file silently downgrades the link to plaintext: no
      authentication and no replay protection.  That file lives on the
      FAT volume which the USB mass-storage gadget also exposes, so its
      absence is a state an attacker can cause.
      When y, boot halts instead of downgrading -- enforced by cargo
      feature `link-auth-enforced`, with no runtime override, mirroring
      SECURE_BOOT_ENFORCED.
      Note the trade-off: a unit that loses its key file will not boot.

config LINK_ENCRYPT_ENFORCED
    # cargo-feature: link-encrypt-enforced
    bool "Refuse any brain-link frame outside an RFC-0019 AEAD session"
    default n
    depends on USE_ROBOT
    help
      Robot domain only (wave 11): the brain link exists only there.
      K-C5.  The per-packet gate in auth_envelope refuses to produce or
      accept a brain-protocol frame unless it travels inside an
      established RFC-0019 AEAD session: no plaintext mode and no
      HMAC-only fallback exist in the build.  Boot also halts when
      /fat/LINK.KEY is missing or invalid (superset of
      LINK_AUTH_ENFORCED): a keyless encrypted-only robot can never
      talk, so the failure surfaces at provisioning, not in the field.
      Enforced by cargo feature `link-encrypt-enforced`, compile-time
      only, no runtime override, mirroring SECURE_BOOT_ENFORCED.
      The handshake runs regardless of the runtime `link_encrypt` key in
      CONFIG.INI — a file on the USB-exposed FAT volume must not be able
      to disarm a compiled-in policy.
config OTA_SIG_MANDATORY
    bool "OTA images must be signed (refuse unsigned updates)" if !BUILD_TYPE_CERTIFIABLE
    default y if BUILD_TYPE_CERTIFIABLE
    default y if !SECURITY_GENERAL
    default n
    help
      When y, the OTA subsystem rejects any firmware image that does
      not carry a valid Ed25519 signature file (KERN_*.SIG on FAT).
      Recommended: set to y whenever SECURE_BOOT_ENFORCED = y so
      the signing chain is unbroken end-to-end.  Starts on for every
      SECURITY posture but general; a BUILD_TYPE_CERTIFIABLE build has it
      on and cannot turn it off.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Link layer encryption (RFC-0019)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

config LINK_AEAD_DEFAULT_ON
    bool "RFC-0019 AEAD link encryption enabled by default"
    default y
    help
      When y, behavior_task runs the RFC-0019 responder handshake
      immediately after TCP Established and wraps all subsequent
      frames in the X25519 + AES-CTR + HMAC envelope.  Both the
      kernel and the brain server MUST agree on this flag.
      Default y since 2026-08-23 (decision del usuario): with a key
      provisioned the link always runs AEAD, which also closes the
      HMAC-only reboot-replay wedge (the brain resets its envelope
      Receiver per AEAD connection).  Without a key the brain link is
      closed, never downgraded to plaintext.  `link_encrypt=0` in
      CONFIG.INI still disables it at runtime; the non-overridable
      variant is LINK_ENCRYPT_ENFORCED.
      Set the runtime key via LINK_PSK_PATH.

config LINK_PSK_PATH
    string "Path to link pre-shared key file on FAT filesystem"
    default "/fat/LINK.KEY"
    help
      FAT-path to the 32-byte PSK used for the Noise_XXpsk0 handshake.
      The file must exist before first boot if LINK_AEAD_DEFAULT_ON=y.
      Rotate annually; overlap period is 24 h (mount new key, reboot,
      confirm both sides are on new key, then remove old).

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PMP / memory protection
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

config PMP_REGIONS_RESERVED
    int "PMP regions reserved for M-mode kernel use"
    range 0 12
    default 4
    help
      Number of RISC-V PMP entries the kernel allocates for its own
      regions (ROM, MMIO, kernel text/data).  The remaining
      (16 - PMP_REGIONS_RESERVED) entries are available for user-process
      isolation.  Must leave headroom; current hardware cap is 16.
      Current codebase: 6 total in use, but 4 is the design budget
      (crates/core/arch-riscv64/src/pmp.rs N_PMP_REGIONS = 6 current impl).

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Signed capability topology (RFC-0005, wave 15 TOPOSIGN)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

choice
    prompt "Capability topology source"
    default TOPOLOGY_SOURCE_SIGNED_REQUIRED if !BOARD_QEMU
    default TOPOLOGY_SOURCE_SIGNED_OR_BUILTIN
    help
      Where the boot takes the capability topology from: the rows every
      ring-3 task's capabilities, scheduler class, priority and memory
      budget come from. The signed form is /fat/CAPS.TOM + /fat/CAPS.SIG
      (a bare Ed25519 signature under the embedded topology key, the key
      CONFIG.SIG uses) and /fat/SCHED.TOM, which CAPS.TOM binds by its
      SHA-256 (`sched_sha256`), together with this device's id and a
      counter (TOPOLOGY_BIND_DEVICE, TOPOLOGY_COUNTER_FLOOR). The built-in
      form is crates/core/topology/src/builder.rs, compiled into the image.
      `make topo-volume TOPO_IMAGE=<img>` writes the built-in topology of a
      given .config and feature set onto an image, bound to that image's
      device (tests/host/topology-tests topo_emit).

      CAPS.SIG is verified before any byte is parsed, and SCHED.TOM's hash
      before SCHED.TOM is parsed; the parsed topology then passes the same
      admission as the built-in one (admission_check, deadline_admission on
      the real CPU count, the RT_BAND_CAP_PCT band, memory) BEFORE it is
      published, so a refused file never becomes the topology.

      Defaults (owner decision, 2026-10-05): "signed required" on every
      product board (BOARD_VF2, BOARD_K1, BOARD_GENERIC: the vf2, k1,
      embedded and fleet profiles), because under signed-or-built-in
      deleting the files from the (USB-exposed) volume boots the built-in
      topology, which may grant more than the deployment's signed one.
      Every product make target builds the volume that carries them,
      signed with the board key: `make vf2` -> build/disk-board.img,
      `make k1` -> build/disk-board-k1.img, `make build-fleet` ->
      build/disk-board-fleet.img; without TOPOLOGY_PRIVKEY_PATH they
      refuse. "Signed if present" on BOARD_QEMU (development and the gate),
      where one disk is booted by kernels with different built-in
      topologies.

      Cost, instructions at the topology install (QEMU virt, -icount
      shift=0, -smp 1, the gate's 19-row topology, 2026-10-05),
      riscv64 / aarch64:
        built-in only (BUILTIN)                        17,800 / 16,376
        no files on the volume (fallback + record)    126,400 / 116,141
        signed set installed (one signature)        1,797,300 / 1,271,881
      The one Ed25519 verification is about 1.35 M of it on riscv64
      (measured with the verification skipped: 443,400); reads, the
      SCHED.TOM hash, the binding, the floor sector, the parse and the
      admission of the candidate are the rest. A first boot of a new
      counter adds the floor write. RAM: the two buffers (see
      TOPOLOGY_CAPS_MAX_KB), under every choice. The cost is printed on the
      "[TOPO] Topology installed: ... in N ticks" line of every boot.

    config TOPOLOGY_SOURCE_BUILTIN
        bool "Built-in only (never read the volume)"
        help
          The topology compiled into the image, as before wave 15. The
          files are not opened: no boot cost, no RAM for the buffers
          (they are still reserved), and no record.

    config TOPOLOGY_SOURCE_SIGNED_OR_BUILTIN
        bool "Signed if present and valid, else built-in"
        help
          The signed topology when all four files are on the volume and
          it verifies, parses and is admitted. With none of the four
          files, the built-in topology, a "[TOPO] WARNING" line and a
          SAFETY_TOPO_SOURCE flight-recorder record (action 1, fallback
          missing). A set that is present but refused (partial, too
          large, bad signature, does not parse, not admitted) is a
          security event: see TOPOLOGY_INVALID.

    config TOPOLOGY_SOURCE_SIGNED_REQUIRED
        bool "Signed required (halt without it)"
        help
          The signed topology or no boot. Missing or refused files write
          a durable SAFETY_TOPO_SOURCE record (action 3 missing, 4
          invalid) and halt the boot hart before any ring-3 task or
          kernel task the topology admits is created. Choose it only
          when every volume this image boots from carries the signed
          files.

endchoice

choice
    prompt "A signed topology that is present but invalid"
    depends on TOPOLOGY_SOURCE_SIGNED_OR_BUILTIN
    default TOPOLOGY_INVALID_FALLBACK
    help
      Under "signed if present and valid": what a set that IS on the
      volume but is refused does. Either way the boot prints a "[TOPO]
      REFUSED" line with the reason and writes a durable
      SAFETY_TOPO_SOURCE record whose detail says which file and step.

      Default fallback: whoever can tamper with the files can also delete
      them, and deletion already boots the built-in topology under this
      source policy, so halting on a tampered file would add a denial of
      service without closing anything. Halt is for a deployment that
      wants a corrupted or tampered volume to stop the machine where it
      can be inspected (with deletion still falling back; for "never
      boot without the signed topology" choose "signed required").

    config TOPOLOGY_INVALID_FALLBACK
        bool "Fall back to the built-in topology"
        help
          Install the built-in topology after the REFUSED line and the
          record (action 2, fallback invalid).

    config TOPOLOGY_INVALID_HALT
        bool "Halt"
        help
          Record (action 4, halt invalid) and halt the boot hart.

endchoice

config TOPOLOGY_BIND_DEVICE
    bool "The signed topology must name this device"
    depends on !TOPOLOGY_SOURCE_BUILTIN
    default y
    help
      CAPS.TOM (format 4) must carry `device = "<32 hex>"` equal to this
      device's id: the device record in reserved tail sector 2
      (tools/device_provision.py), the id CONFIG.SIG v2 is bound to. A file
      signed for another device is refused (SAFETY_TOPO_SOURCE detail step
      8), a file with no `device` key too (step 10), and so is any file on a
      device with no record (step 11). With n, `device` is optional, and
      still checked when present.

      Default y for every profile: `make` writes the image's own id into the
      files it signs (QEMU images and board volumes alike). Cost: a 16-byte
      compare and one sector read at boot (the record is already read for
      CONFIG.INI; it is read again here), either ISA.

config TOPOLOGY_COUNTER_FLOOR
    bool "Refuse a signed topology older than the last one accepted"
    depends on !TOPOLOGY_SOURCE_BUILTIN
    default y
    help
      CAPS.TOM (format 4) must carry `counter = N` (N >= 1), and N must be at
      least the floor this device keeps in reserved tail sector
      TOPOLOGY_FLOOR_SECTOR. Accepting a file with a higher counter raises
      the floor (one sector write and a flush, once per new counter). An
      older, validly signed CAPS.TOM put back on the volume is refused as a
      replay (SAFETY_TOPO_SOURCE detail step 9, low bits the counter). The
      CONFIG.SIG v2 semantics: the floor only moves up, and a missing or
      invalid floor record reads as 0.

      What it does not stop: whoever can rewrite the reserved tail (a card
      reader, not the USB export) can reset the floor (RFC-0054 §6.3).

      Default y for every profile. Cost: one sector read per boot, either
      ISA.

config TOPOLOGY_FLOOR_SECTOR
    int "Reserved tail sector of the topology counter floor"
    range 3 7
    default 3
    help
      Index in the reserved tail past the FAT32 volume
      (kernel/src/msc_gadget.rs, 8 sectors) of the "KTPF" floor record
      (crates/core/topology/src/device_record.rs). 0 is the link key, 1 the
      entropy seed, 2 the device record; 3..7 are free. Change it only to
      move it off a sector another feature of your build claims, and give
      tools/device_provision.py --topo-sector the same value.

config TOPOLOGY_CAPS_MAX_KB
    int "Largest CAPS.TOM the boot reads (KiB)"
    range 1 4096
    default 8   if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 16  if PROFILE_EDGE
    default 256 if PROFILE_FLEET
    help
      The buffer CAPS.TOM is read into (kernel/src/boot/topology.rs). It
      stays allocated for the life of the kernel: every task name, class
      name and grant target of the installed topology borrows from it. A
      larger file is refused unread (SignedRefusal::TooLarge).

      Cost: this many KiB of .bss on riscv64 and aarch64 alike, whether or
      not the volume carries the file (also under TOPOLOGY_SOURCE_BUILTIN).
      Size it from your file: the QEMU gate's topology (19 rows, 53
      grants, its test rows included) is 4.6 KB; a row is about 100 bytes
      plus 50 per grant. Fleet's 256 KiB leaves room for about 2,000 rows.

config TOPOLOGY_SCHED_MAX_KB
    int "Largest SCHED.TOM the boot reads (KiB)"
    range 1 1024
    default 2  if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 4  if PROFILE_EDGE
    default 16 if PROFILE_FLEET
    help
      The buffer SCHED.TOM is read into; kept for the kernel's life (the
      class names borrow from it). A larger file is refused unread.

      Cost: this many KiB of .bss, either ISA. The QEMU gate's SCHED.TOM
      (5 classes) is under 1 KB; a class is about 190 bytes.

config TOPOLOGY_NEVERALLOW
    bool "Refuse a topology that breaks a neverallow rule"
    default y
    help
      The rules of crates/core/topology/src/neverallow.rs state grants no
      topology may make, whatever its file says: no ring-3 row holds an
      actuator unless the table lists the row as one, no desktop image
      grants a motor, no row both asks the name server for names and serves
      an endpoint or a driver. A topology that breaks one is refused by
      `admission_check` (the signed candidate falls back or halts as
      TOPOLOGY_SOURCE says; the built-in topology halts) and a decision
      record (`neverallow`, /proc/decisions) names the rule and the row.
      `make check-neverallow` runs the same rules over every preset's
      topology, with this option ignored.

      With n the rules are not applied at boot or by `admission_check`.
      Cost: one pass over the rows' grants at install, either ISA, and a
      few hundred bytes of rule table.

config TOPOLOGY_DRIVETRAIN
    bool "The built-in topology may declare the drivetrain"
    depends on !USE_DESKTOP
    default y
    help
      With the `profile-actuation` fixture (the QEMU board's), the
      built-in topology grants `motor.0` and `motor.1` to the row that
      drives the robot. A desktop image never does: the neverallow rule
      `desktop-grants-no-motor` refuses a topology that grants a motor
      there, so this option is hidden (and n) under USE_DESKTOP rather
      than leaving the combination to be refused at boot.

      Cost: none; two grants of the autorun row, either ISA.

config TOPOLOGY_MAX_CLASSES
    int "Scheduler classes a topology may declare"
    range 1 255
    default 8
    help
      Size of the class table of the topology
      (crates/core/topology/src/types.rs, MAX_CLASSES). A SCHED.TOM with
      more classes is refused by the parser. The class count is kept in a
      u8, hence the range.

      Cost: 32 B of RAM per class (the topology slot, .data) and 132 B of
      stack per class in deadline admission (a u32 per class for each of
      its 32 admission CPUs, plus the budget), either ISA. The built-in
      topology declares 5.

config TOPOLOGY_MAX_PIPELINES
    int "[pipeline.NAME] sections a topology may declare"
    range 1 255
    default 8
    help
      Size of the pipeline table (MAX_PIPELINES): each declared pipeline
      adds its dma_kb to the boot DMA pool (DMA_POOL_FLOOR_KB). More are
      refused. Kept in a u8.

      Cost: 24 B of RAM per entry, either ISA.

config TOPOLOGY_MAX_DEVICE_ROWS
    int "[device.NAME] rows a topology may declare"
    range 1 255
    default 16 if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 32
    help
      Size of the device row table (MAX_DEVICE_ROWS, CAPS.TOML format 6):
      each `[device.NAME]` row selects hardware the bus detects (`match =
      "pci:1af4:1041"`, `"dt:ns16550a"`, `"virtio:2"`, `"pciclass:010802:
      ffffff"`) and says what to do with it: admit it, admit it with another
      native driver (`driver = "..."`), or refuse it (`admit = false`). With
      HWBUS_AUTOBIND_TOPOLOGY a device no row admits is detected and not
      bound. More rows are refused. Kept in a u8.

      Cost: 56 B of RAM per entry, either ISA.

config TOPOLOGY_MAX_IMPORTS
    int "Names one task row may import from the name server"
    range 1 64
    default 8
    help
      The `imports = [...]` list of a [task.NAME] row (CAPS.TOML format 5):
      the endpoint names the name server (NAMESD.ELF) may hand that row. A
      row with more is refused by the parser. The list is the row's whole
      claim on the name space: a name it does not list is not answered.

      Cost: none by itself; the pool is TOPOLOGY_IMPORTS_TOTAL.

config TOPOLOGY_IMPORTS_TOTAL
    int "Imports declared across all rows of a topology"
    range 1 4096
    default 32
    help
      Size of the imports pool (types.rs, IMPORTS_TOTAL): every name any row
      imports takes one entry. More are refused. The built-in topology
      declares 1 (the shell imports the terminal).

      Cost: 16 B of RAM per entry (a name slice, .data), either ISA.

config TOPOLOGY_MAX_SIGNERS
    int "Package signers one topology may admit"
    range 1 64
    default 4
    help
      The `[signer.NAME]` sections of a format-6 CAPS.TOM: the device policy
      of signed application packages (crates/core/pkg). A package installs
      only when its author's key is one of these; a topology with more is
      refused by the parser. The policy is signed with the rest of the
      topology, so the device owner's key is the only root of trust and a
      package cannot add a signer for itself.

      Cost: 80 B of RAM per entry (.bss in the installer, .data in the
      kernel), either ISA.

config TOPOLOGY_SIGNER_WARRANTS_TOTAL
    int "Warrant ceilings declared across all signers"
    range 1 1024
    default 32
    help
      Size of the pool behind the signers' `warrants = ["kind:prefix", ...]`
      lists (types.rs, SIGNER_WARRANTS_TOTAL): the ceiling of what each
      signer's packages may ask for. A package row holding a grant no entry
      of its signer covers is refused. More entries than the pool holds are
      refused by the parser.

      Cost: 16 B of RAM per entry, either ISA.
config TOPOLOGY_MAX_PEERS
    int "[peer.NAME] sections (classes of remote nodes) a topology may declare"
    range 1 16
    default 4
    help
      Size of the peer table (types.rs, MAX_PEERS), CAPS.TOML format 6: a
      class is a set of node ids an `export` of the RPC server's row may name
      (`export = ["decisions@fleet"]`). More are refused.

      Cost: 48 B of RAM per class, either ISA.

config TOPOLOGY_PEER_NODES
    int "Nodes one peer class may list"
    range 1 16
    default 4
    help
      The `nodes = [...]` list of a [peer.NAME] section. A node belongs to one
      class only. More are refused. Cost: 4 B per node per class, either ISA.

config TOPOLOGY_EXPORTS_TOTAL
    int "Export entries across the RPC server's row"
    range 1 256
    default 16
    help
      Size of the exports pool (types.rs, EXPORTS_TOTAL): every NAME@CLASS of
      the `export` key takes one entry. More are refused. Nothing leaves a
      node except through one of these entries.

      Cost: 32 B of RAM per entry (two name slices), either ISA.

config NAMES_LEND_TTL_MS
    int "Lifetime of a capability the name server lends (ms, 0 = the server's own)"
    range 0 86400000
    default 0 if PROFILE_EMBEDDED
    default 600000 if PROFILE_FLEET
    default 3600000
    help
      The name server (NAMESD.ELF) stamps every endpoint it lends with
      `now + this`: the transfer descriptor's `expiry_ns`, which the kernel
      turns into the deadline of the borrower's copy in the warrant tree (the
      earlier of it and the server's own, so a lease never outlives its
      parent). When it passes, a call through the copy fails as stale and the
      borrower asks the name server again (azos_names::Renewing). 0 lends
      with no deadline of its own: the copy lives as long as the server's
      capability, which a static wiring (EMBEDDED) has no use to shorten.

      A shorter lease bounds how long a revoked or re-routed service stays
      reachable through an old copy; each renewal is one more name-server
      call per lease, so a very short one costs a lookup every few calls.
      Cost: no RAM; one clock read per lookup. The same on every ISA: it is a
      wall-clock lifetime, not a cycle count.

config TOPOLOGY_NAME_MAX
    int "Longest task, class or pipeline name in a topology (bytes)"
    range 8 255
    default 32
    help
      MAX_TASK_NAME_LEN: the longest section name ([task.NAME],
      [class.NAME], [pipeline.NAME]), class reference and enum word the
      parser accepts; longer is refused (NameTooLong). Names borrow from
      the file buffer, so this costs no RAM; it bounds the parser's scans.

config TOPOLOGY_TARGET_MAX
    int "Longest grant target in a topology (bytes)"
    range 16 255
    default 64
    help
      MAX_TARGET_LEN: the longest `target = "..."` of a grant (a channel
      path, an image name, "disk.part.1"); longer is refused. No RAM cost
      (targets borrow from the file buffer).

config TOPOLOGY_INLINE_FIELDS_MAX
    int "Fields in one grant's inline table"
    range 4 32
    default 8
    help
      MAX_INLINE_FIELDS: how many `key = value` pairs one `{ ... }` grant
      may hold (kind, target, perm, transfer are the four in use). More
      are refused. Cost: 32 B of parser stack per field, either ISA.

choice
    prompt "CPU entropy instruction as a pool seed source"
    default ENTROPY_CPU_SOURCE_CREDIT
    help
      The CPU's own entropy instruction seeds the kernel entropy pool
      (kernel/src/boot/entropy.rs `install_entropy`, after virtio-rng and
      independent of it): riscv64 Zkr `seed` CSR (RV_ZKR), aarch64 FEAT_RNG
      RNDRRS (A64_RNG), x86_64 RDSEED, else RDRAND (X86_RDSEED,
      X86_RDRAND). Each instruction is used only where its own extension
      choice allows it and the boot probe finds it; without one the boot
      is exactly as before. The pool seeds the TCP ISN secret, the
      DHCP/DNS/NTP ids, the link keys, ring 3's `entropy` endpoint and
      the stack canary; without any credited source they stay on their
      runtime-counter paths.

      credit: mixed and credited, so the CPU alone seeds the pool on a
      board with no entropy device (Linux RANDOM_TRUST_CPU=y). mix-only:
      mixed, never credited; the pool still needs virtio-rng or the
      persisted seed (Linux RANDOM_TRUST_CPU=n). off: never read.

      Cost: one draw of 48 bytes at boot (RDSEED/RNDRRS: a few hundred
      cycles per 8 bytes; Zkr: 16 bits per CSR read); nothing after.

    config ENTROPY_CPU_SOURCE_CREDIT
        bool "credit: mix and credit (trust the CPU)"
    config ENTROPY_CPU_SOURCE_MIX
        bool "mix-only: mix, never credit"
    config ENTROPY_CPU_SOURCE_OFF
        bool "off: never read the CPU's entropy instruction"
endchoice

config ENTROPY_CPU_RETRIES
    int "CPU entropy instruction: retries per word"
    range 1 100000
    default 1000
    help
      How many times the boot retries one word of the CPU entropy
      instruction when it reports no data (x86 CF=0, aarch64 NZCV.Z=1,
      riscv64 BIST/WAIT) before it gives the source up for this boot, with
      the ISA's pause hint between tries. RDSEED and RNDRRS wait for a
      fresh conditioner output and can fail many times in a row under
      load; Intel's 10-retry guidance is for RDRAND only. Bounded either
      way: a dead source costs this many tries once, never a hang.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Ring-3 allocator (userspace/lib/ualloc)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The knobs of the hardened allocator native ring-3 programs link
# (`azos_ualloc`, Scudo's shape: size-class blocks, randomized chunk
# placement, checksummed chunk headers, a quarantine of freed chunks, guard
# pages). They are plain integers and bools read through `azos_limits`, not
# cargo features: the allocator is a userspace library, not a kernel feature.
# Defaults follow the SECURITY posture: every posture but `general` starts
# with the quarantine, the free-chunk poison and the randomized placement on.

config USER_ALLOC_QUARANTINE_KB
    int "Ring-3 allocator: quarantine of freed small chunks, KiB (0 = off)"
    range 0 4096
    default 64 if !SECURITY_GENERAL
    default 0
    help
      Total slot bytes the allocator holds back after a free before a chunk
      can be handed out again. A chunk in quarantine keeps its header in the
      "quarantined" state, so a second free of it is a detected double free,
      and (USER_ALLOC_POISON_FREED) a write through a stale pointer is
      detected when the chunk leaves quarantine. Chunks leave in random
      order when USER_ALLOC_RANDOMIZE is on, oldest first otherwise. 0
      turns the quarantine off: a freed chunk is reusable at once, and a
      double free is detected only until its chunk is allocated again.
      Cost: the bytes are resident while held (Scudo's default is 256 KiB
      per thread cache, this is per process). Large allocations are never
      quarantined: they are unmapped on free and their address range is
      never reused (the kernel's mmap only moves the break up and brk does
      not shrink it; a kernel that lowered the break would void this), so a
      stale pointer into one faults.

config USER_ALLOC_QUARANTINE_SLOTS
    int "Ring-3 allocator: quarantine entries"
    range 8 4096
    default 128
    help
      How many freed chunks the quarantine can hold (the table is static,
      8 bytes each). A free that finds the table full evicts one chunk
      first, whatever the byte budget says.

config USER_ALLOC_BLOCK_KB
    int "Ring-3 allocator: size-class block, KiB"
    range 8 1024
    default 16
    help
      How much anonymous memory a size class takes from the kernel each
      time it runs out of chunks. The kernel charges a mapping against the
      program's frame budget whole, when it is made, so a small block keeps
      a program with a tight `mem_pages` row inside it; a big one makes
      fewer mmap calls. Rounded up to the page size.

config USER_ALLOC_MAX_MAPS
    int "Ring-3 allocator: live mappings (blocks + large allocations)"
    range 16 4096
    default 256
    help
      Capacity of the table the allocator checks every freed pointer
      against (16 bytes each). A block or large allocation that does not
      fit fails the allocation (null), it is never untracked.

config USER_ALLOC_RANDOMIZE
    bool "Ring-3 allocator: randomize chunk placement"
    default y if !SECURITY_GENERAL
    default n
    help
      Which free chunk an allocation takes, the order a fresh block is
      handed out in, the position of a large allocation inside its pages
      and the quarantine eviction order come from the kernel entropy pool
      (SYS_ENTROPY_READ_TYPED, so the program's row needs the `entropy`
      capability). Without it the allocator is deterministic: LIFO reuse,
      fresh chunks in address order, large allocations ending exactly at
      their guard page (the tightest overflow detection). A program
      without the capability, or on a boot whose pool is unseeded, runs
      deterministic and prints one line saying so, unless
      USER_ALLOC_SEED_REQUIRED.

config USER_ALLOC_SEED_REQUIRED
    bool "Ring-3 allocator: fault when no kernel entropy is available"
    default y if SECURITY_SEPARATION_STATIC || SECURITY_CONFIDENTIAL
    default n
    help
      The header checksum is keyed and the placement randomized from one
      read of the entropy pool at the first allocation. With this on, a
      program that cannot get it (no `entropy` capability, unseeded pool)
      takes a recorded fault there instead of running with a key and a
      placement an attacker can predict.

config USER_ALLOC_POISON_FREED
    bool "Ring-3 allocator: poison freed chunks and check the poison"
    default y if !SECURITY_GENERAL
    default n
    help
      A freed chunk's first 512 bytes are filled with a fixed byte and
      checked when it leaves quarantine (or, without quarantine, when it is
      handed out again): a write through a stale pointer is a recorded
      fault, not silent corruption. Cost: a fill and a compare of up to 512
      bytes per free.

config USER_ALLOC_GUARD_PAGES
    bool "Ring-3 allocator: guard pages around blocks and large allocations"
    default y
    help
      Every block and every large allocation is mapped between two
      PROT_NONE reservations (mmap with prot 0: the range is reserved, no
      page is mapped, a touch kills the task). Costs address space and two
      extra mmap calls per mapping, no frames. Off: blocks and large
      allocations are mapped back to back.

# ── Native SDK runtime (sdk/, crates/core/stdrt) ─────────────────────────────
# A plain integer read by `make sdk` (tools/kconfig_to_cargo.py --sdk-env), like
# the allocator knobs above, whose engine `std::alloc::System` shares.

config SDK_THREAD_STACK_KB
    int "Native SDK: default stack of a std thread, KiB"
    range 16 4096
    default 128
    help
      The stack `std::thread::spawn` gives a thread when the caller names no
      size. The kernel charges a mapping against the program's frame budget
      whole, when it is made, so a program with a tight `mem_pages` row wants
      this small; a deep recursion wants it large. Rounded up to the page
      size, with a guard page below and above.

# MITIGATION_SPECTRE_V1_INDEX, ML_DATA_SIG_REQUIRED and CONSOLE_LOCKDOWN moved
# to the Mitigations menu (config/Kconfig.mitigations), wave 11.

endmenu # Security
